WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.456
The paper makes four contributions.

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:06.992
First,

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:10.528
it defines a semantics-aware notion of adversarial
robustness for node-level predictions,

00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:14.064
and with it a brand new concept for the graph
domain called over-robustness,

00:00:14.144 --> 00:00:17.600
which is robustness against admissible
perturbations whose ground-truth label has already changed.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.136
Second,

00:00:21.216 --> 00:00:24.672
using Contextual Stochastic Block Models it shows
that common perturbation sets contain

00:00:24.752 --> 00:00:28.208
a large fraction of graphs with changed semantics
and that every examined GNN is significantly

00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:31.744
over-robust,

00:00:31.824 --> 00:00:35.280
with matching patterns on real-world graphs.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.816
Third,

00:00:38.896 --> 00:00:42.352
it shows that folding the known label structure
into inference through label propagation

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:45.888
significantly reduces over-robustness with no cost
to accuracy or adversarial robustness.

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:49.424
Fourth,

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:52.960
it proves that classifying an inductively sampled
node carries no robustness-accuracy tradeoff.
